\section{Motivation: Simple GPU-Friendly Approximation of Spiky Gradient Structure}
\label{sec:motivation}

Large transformer models exhibit a striking empirical regularity: during training,
the gradients of large linear layers (e.g., $W_q$, $W_k$, $W_v$, and $W_o$) are neither
random nor isotropic.
Instead, a small number of directions consistently dominate the gradient energy,
while the remaining directions are highly diverse and context-dependent.
In other words, transformer gradients are strongly \emph{spiky} and effectively
low-dimensional.

\paragraph{The computational challenge.}
From a classical numerical linear algebra perspective, extracting low-dimensional
structure from a matrix naturally suggests singular value decomposition (SVD) or
related orthogonalization-based methods.
However, SVD requires iterative QR decompositions, reorthogonalization, and global
synchronization.
These operations are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item difficult to implement efficiently on GPUs,
    \item communication-heavy in distributed training,
    \item impractical to invoke repeatedly inside training loops.
\end{itemize}
As a result, despite the presence of strong low-rank structure, SVD-based techniques
are rarely used directly during large-scale neural network training.

\paragraph{What structure do we actually need?}
Crucially, our goal is \emph{not} to compute an optimal low-rank approximation in the
classical sense.
Instead, we seek to identify the \emph{dominant shared subspace} that governs
optimization dynamics.
This subspace corresponds to grammar-like or structural effects that:
\begin{itemize}
    \item recur across tokens and batches,
    \item survive aggregation and averaging,
    \item dominate training dynamics despite occupying only a few dimensions.
\end{itemize}
Once this dominant subspace is identified, fine-grained reconstruction of the full
gradient matrix is unnecessary.

\paragraph{Design principle: hardware-native simplicity.}
The central thesis of this chapter is that the dominant low-dimensional structure in
transformer gradient matrices can be approximated using \emph{extremely simple
operations} that are native to modern GPU hardware.
In particular, we show that:
\begin{itemize}
    \item basic averaging operations (matrix--vector products),
    \item and their natural generalization via a small number of random probes
    (matrix--matrix products),
\end{itemize}
are sufficient to isolate the spiky shared structure under mild and interpretable
assumptions.

These operations:
\begin{itemize}
    \item avoid QR decompositions and explicit orthogonalization,
    \item map directly to highly optimized GPU kernels,
    \item incur negligible overhead relative to standard training steps.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Scope of this chapter.}
This chapter develops a simple but rigorous framework to justify this approach.
We:
\begin{itemize}
    \item formalize when averaging suppresses semantic variability while preserving
    shared structure,
    \item prove that averaging-based estimators recover dominant rank-one components,
    \item generalize the method to low-dimensional ($k$-rank) subspaces using random
    probes,
    \item and connect these ideas to practical implications for transformer
    optimization.
\end{itemize}

The overarching message is that \emph{simple, GPU-friendly primitives are sufficient
to approximate the dominant structure that matters for optimization}, making
low-rank and subspace-aware methods practical at scale.